ices ["q-117250/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-117250/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-117250/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-117250/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-117250/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117250" :question/id #uuid "9985084e-755c-51fb-80a8-a70831820a0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117250" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,\\pi )$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117250/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117250"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65828"] :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-70768/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7c1eff62-faf2-5395-aa7f-3b7b0292053e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70768/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "613ced06-390c-5b5b-9d6e-00f71f42e411" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70768/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "0ac94549-6189-537c-ba55-86ac70c3d2bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70768/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "08484c2f-fd25-5b8c-925c-5c77f863525f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70768/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "78791bf3-487f-5615-adc8-b32dbb04c37d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70768/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b858c286-6eb2-59cc-8787-9e1c2b27e881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-70768/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-70768/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-70768/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8