nge of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\pi $ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\pi )$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-70768/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70768"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "62f014d8-75ba-50d8-a497-0c0a161df040" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fdfa3991-6db6-5b3e-bda4-fb8e9e58cc99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "218ac60f-0d53-570c-b372-6802a12599c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b2e08124-5234-5e9b-99bd-19db73a7da14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bac5342b-5d10-547c-86b0-c690a8a29394" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ca9703-0ffa-504a-81c5-5a2704135141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-70769/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-70769/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-70769/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-70769/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-70769/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-70769/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70769" :question/id #uuid "3692464e-f017-5c85-befd-6c0f45c59ad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70769" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-70769/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70769"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "79fec1fd-e877-52fb-a2ee-65e36e130a06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5e990c7c-0abe-5fc7-abb7-f4a114a4f1ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "6ab0fb69-d4a4-5c1b-9020-5adfdd25b043" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "afeac6e4-0683-5840-98af-d8cf9a3ed1dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e7037df1-b25e-5678-bf62-3023c3b0c8e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcfa2ada-c6f3-58f7-bf88-c6f9de680bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-70770/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-70770/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-70770/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-70770/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-70770/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-70770/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-70770" :question/id #uuid "2cacc97b-4c53-5c23-a1c9-c837345342b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-70770" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-70770/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70770"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
